---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Experiment from '../../components/ScientificWorldExperiment.astro';
import Modules from '../../components/ScientificWorldModules.astro';
import Lab from '../../components/ScientificWorldLab.astro';
import Prompts from '../../components/ScientificWorldPrompts.astro';
import replayFrames from '../../data/scientific-worlds/teaching-figure.json';
import score from '../../data/scientific-worlds/scorecard.json';
import bibtex from '../../data/scientific-worlds/citation.bib?raw';
import { writing } from '../../data/writing.js';

const entry = writing.find(item => item.href === '/writing/scientific-worlds/')!;
const repo = 'https://github.com/BeibaiDraco/scientific-worlds-benchmark';
const release = repo + '/blob/fa0c8028b2b4fe718acba6bd956c9059415fd59e';
const citationUrl = repo + '/tree/v' + score.version;
// Device zero in the released teaching replay uses relabelled switch addresses.
const replayWire = { source: 481, destination: 378, inverted: true };
const { M } = score.conditions;
---
<Base title={entry.title} description={entry.description} current="Writing" article={true}
  author="Draco (Yunlong) Xu" publishedTime={entry.date} updated="November 19, 2026"
  socialImage={{...entry.image,type:'image/png',alt:'The Measure of Worlds v1.0 · Virtual Brain. Eight wiring rules in a grid of 32 switches discovered by Astra 6.0 Max.'}}>
  <link slot="head" rel="alternate" type="application/x-bibtex" href="/writing/scientific-worlds/citation.bib" title="Benchmark citation"/>
  <article class="bay report"><div class="wide sheet">
    <header class="opening">
      <h1><span class="series">The Measure of Worlds <span>v1.0</span></span>Virtual Brain</h1>
      <p class="byline">Draco (Yunlong) Xu · <time datetime={entry.date}>November 17, 2026</time> · <a href="#results">Astra’s results</a> · <a href="#cite">Cite</a></p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lead">A virtual brain has 720 switches and 20 lights. We give an AI eight copies and a pattern of lights to hold steady on each. The switches will flip at random. What could the AI measure now that would let it control the lights later?</p>
      <p>In the lab, the AI can flip switches together and pay to see which lights change. Then it must build eight controllers. Holding every switch fixed would cost too much. The targets are ours. Which switches to study together, and what to measure about them, are decisions the AI has to make.</p>

      <h2>Who chose the variables in <i>F = ma</i>?</h2>
      <p>Two carts pass you at the same speed. One is rolling steadily. The other is speeding up. To relate their motion to force, you need a quantity that distinguishes them. Measure how their velocity changes and you have acceleration. By the time a textbook gives you force, mass and acceleration, someone has already done enough science to know what belongs in those three columns.</p>
      <p>AI Feynman asks for an equation that fits the supplied measurements. DiscoveryWorld lets an AI design experiments and checks for discoveries its authors anticipated. Here we leave the useful quantity unnamed. <strong>Can an AI invent a column worth adding to the table?</strong> Our premise is that the ultimate test of a scientific idea is what it lets us change. Anyone can test Astra’s answer by running the machine its discovery made possible.</p>
    </div>

    <section class="prose results" id="results" aria-labelledby="results-title">
      <h2 id="results-title">What made 32 switches one thing?</h2>
      <p>Astra 6.0 Max knew the rule for how four switches interact. It had to decide whether any larger group was worth studying. In one successful attempt it found 32 switches arranged in a grid. Each square of four neighbors contributed +1 or −1. Adding those contributions gave Astra its new variable. Switch patterns with the same total had the same effect on the lights, however different the patterns looked.</p>
      <p>That gave Astra a cheaper way to build. It wired eight switches so that each copied another switch or did the opposite. With those pairs linked, the pluses and minuses always balanced. The other 24 switches could flip freely without this grid disturbing a single light. The eight connections Astra recorded during research appear in every controller it built. Of its 5,200 credits, it had two left when the machines were ready.</p>
      <p class="finding">With the four-switch law (M), Astra delivered all eight controllers in <strong>{M.successes} of {M.sessions} attempts ({M.percent}%)</strong> across 19 worlds. Both wins came on the same world, tried seven times. Each winning controller held its target through 100,000 random inputs.</p>
    </section>

    <Experiment frames={replayFrames} exampleWire={replayWire} replayUrl={release+'/docs/method.md#the-replay'}/>
    <Modules/>
    <Lab/>

    <Prompts release={release}/>

    <section class="prose" id="why-build" aria-labelledby="why-build-title">
      <h2 id="why-build-title">How do you reward a question nobody assigned?</h2>
      <p>In a real lab, years can pass before an idea lets anyone do something new. In the meantime, we ask experts which questions look promising. A virtual world shortens that wait. We run the machines. If all eight hold their targets through 100,000 random inputs apiece and there is money left to start them, the score is 1. Otherwise it is 0. A shortcut found by trial and error earns the same point. The research record lets us see which ideas actually went into the design.</p>
      <p>That distinction matters for training as well. Today the score measures what Astra could do. In reinforcement learning, the same outcome could reward the choices that led to it, across many attempts. A model could learn <em>which questions to ask</em> from the things its discoveries let it do, even when no human has written down the question or its answer.</p>
    </section>

    <footer class="endnotes prose">
      <p><a class="run" href={repo}>Run Virtual Brain ↗</a><a href={repo+'/blob/main/docs/method.md'}>Scoring and research records</a></p>
      <p class="sources">The ideas behind the task · <a href="/writing/scientific-understanding/">What counts as understanding?</a> · <a href="/writing/scientific-value/">What makes a discovery valuable?</a></p>
      <p class="sources">Related work · <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.11481">AI Feynman</a> · <a href="https://allenai.github.io/discoveryworld/">DiscoveryWorld</a> · <a href="https://www.cs.columbia.edu/~bchen/neural-state-variables/">Discovering variables from video</a></p>
    </footer>
    <section class="citation prose" id="cite" aria-labelledby="cite-title">
      <h2 id="cite-title">Cite this benchmark</h2>
      <p>Xu, Y. (2026). <i>The Measure of Worlds: Virtual Brain</i> (Version {score.version}) [Benchmark software]. <a href={citationUrl}>{citationUrl}</a></p>
      <details><summary>BibTeX</summary><pre tabindex="0"><code>{bibtex.trimEnd()}</code></pre><button hidden type="button" data-copy-bib>Copy BibTeX</button><span role="status" class="copy-status"></span></details>
      <a class="download" href="/writing/scientific-worlds/citation.bib" download="measure-of-worlds-v1.bib">Download .bib</a>
    </section>
  </div></article>
</Base>
<script>
  const copy = document.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-copy-bib]');
  if (copy && navigator.clipboard?.writeText) {
    copy.hidden = false;
    const cite = copy.closest('details')!;
    copy.addEventListener('click', async () => {
      const status = cite.querySelector('[role="status"]')!;
      try {
        await navigator.clipboard.writeText(cite.querySelector('code')!.textContent!);
        status.textContent = 'Copied.';
      } catch {
        status.textContent = 'Select the text above to copy it.';
        cite.querySelector('pre')!.focus();
      }
    });
  }
</script>
<style>
  .sheet {--reading-width:54rem;width:100%;margin-inline:auto;padding-inline:clamp(12px,1.4vw,24px)}
  .opening,.prose {max-width:var(--reading-width);margin-inline:auto}
  .opening {padding-top:clamp(36px,5vw,70px)}
  .byline,.endnotes {font-family:var(--mono)}
  h1 {margin:0;color:var(--sumi);font:390 clamp(43px,5.1vw,74px)/1.02 var(--serif);letter-spacing:-.045em;text-wrap:balance}
  .series {display:block;font:450 clamp(22px,2.5vw,32px)/1.2 var(--serif);letter-spacing:-.02em;margin-bottom:8px;color:var(--wave)}
  .series span {font:14px var(--mono);letter-spacing:0;white-space:nowrap}
  .byline {font-size:11px;line-height:1.6;color:var(--sea);margin:18px 0 29px;padding-bottom:18px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--kei)}
  a {text-underline-offset:.18em}
  .byline a,.lead i {white-space:nowrap}
  .prose {font:20px/1.52 var(--serif);color:var(--contour)}
  .prose p {margin:0 0 15px;font:inherit}
  .prose .lead {font:400 clamp(23px,2.35vw,31px)/1.30 var(--serif);color:var(--sumi);letter-spacing:-.015em}
  h2 {font:450 clamp(29px,3vw,38px)/1.12 var(--serif);letter-spacing:-.025em;color:var(--sumi);margin:33px 0 12px;text-wrap:balance}
  .results {scroll-margin-top:18px}
  .prose .finding {padding-left:15px;border-left:3px solid var(--wave)}
  .endnotes {border-top:1px solid var(--kei);padding:17px 0 10px;margin-top:28px;font:12px/1.65 var(--mono)}
  .endnotes p:first-child {display:flex;gap:3px 22px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:8px}
  .endnotes p.sources {font:15px/1.5 var(--serif);color:var(--sea)}
  .endnotes .run {color:var(--sumi)}
  .citation {border-top:1px solid var(--kei);padding:18px 0 22px;scroll-margin-top:20px}
  .citation h2 {margin:0 0 9px;font-size:26px}.citation p {font-size:17px;margin:0 0 10px}
  .citation details,.download {font:13px/1.5 var(--mono)}
  .citation summary {width:fit-content;cursor:pointer;color:var(--wave);padding:5px 0}
  .citation pre {font:13px/1.55 var(--mono);white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-wrap:anywhere;padding:12px 15px;background:var(--shoal);margin:4px 0 9px}
  .citation p a {overflow-wrap:anywhere}
  .citation button {font:inherit;color:var(--wave);background:var(--paper);border:1px solid var(--wave);padding:4px 8px;cursor:pointer}
  .copy-status {margin-inline-start:8px}
  .download {display:inline-block;padding:4px 0}
  @media(max-width:740px){.sheet{padding:0}.opening{padding-top:30px}.prose{font-size:19px;line-height:1.47}.byline{margin:14px 0 20px}.endnotes{font-size:12px}}
</style>
